<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pío Romero Bosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pío Romero Bosque was president of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> from 1927 to 1931. Chosen as successor by the Meléndez–Quiñónez family, he broke with it and allowed freer political activity. The presidential election of 1931, won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo">Arturo Araujo</a>, is regarded as the country's first free election, but it was followed within months by a military coup.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque.jpg" alt="Pío Romero Bosque" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pacific and Atlantic (Agence photographique). Agence photographique pour le document reproduit (commanditaire) Agence... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr_Pio_Romero_Bosque,_pr%C3%A9sident_de_Salvador_(Pacific)_-_btv1b532073706_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
